WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.470
Hey guys, it's Jan here!

00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:06.529
I've been to see Batman v Superman: Dawn of
Justice and I've got lots of thoughts on the

00:00:06.529 --> 00:00:07.529
movie.

00:00:07.529 --> 00:00:11.040
But before I get to those, I wanna let you
know about a great giveaway I've got for a

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:16.309
very cool Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice:
The Art of the Film hardback book.

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:21.370
It'll be on my BvS Easter Egg video, so make
sure you subscribe and I'll add a link to

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:23.400
that video here when it's ready!

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.880
Ok, the big-screen schedule that DC has set
themselves, with solo movies and team-ups

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:33.489
like Justice League, is very ambitious, and
before seeing Batman v Superman I did wonder

00:00:33.489 --> 00:00:38.399
if DC was trying to run before they could
walk, and whether they were rushing too much

00:00:38.399 --> 00:00:42.940
to keep up with Marvel, who took more time
to build up their big-screen empire with a

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:46.359
shed-load of movies and a massive universe
of characters and worlds.

00:00:46.359 --> 00:00:51.379
However, now I've seen Batman v Superman,
I feel that DC's ambition looks like it could

00:00:51.379 --> 00:00:52.780
pay off rather nicely.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:57.649
In BvS, they successfully introduce Ben Affleck's
Batman, and they give us a taste of what we

00:00:57.649 --> 00:00:59.879
can expect from Gal Gadot's Wonder Woman movie
too.

00:00:59.879 --> 00:01:04.989
There is certainly a hell of a lot to fit
into this film, what with all the elements

00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:09.570
we expect of a Superman movie and a Batman
movie having to have their moment.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:13.799
Not to mention the pointers to various members
of the Justice League, which are somewhat

00:01:13.799 --> 00:01:15.170
shoehorned in.

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:19.520
Still, DC's world and franchise-building feels
like it's off to a decent start and I have

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:22.409
my fingers crossed that will continue in future
films.

00:01:22.409 --> 00:01:27.439
I can definitely get on board with Ben Affleck's
believably world-weary rendition of the Dark

00:01:27.439 --> 00:01:32.240
Knight, and it will certainly help wipe his
Daredevil a little further from my memory.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:37.310
In fact, Affleck's presence on screen puts
Henry Cavill's Superman firmly in the shade.

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:42.530
I look forward to seeing Batfleck in a solo
movie, along with more of Jeremy Irons' sardonic

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:43.880
turn as Alfred.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:47.590
As for Gal Gadot, I enjoyed her appearance
as Wonder Woman and it's certainly piqued

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:50.060
my interest for her solo outing next year.

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:54.490
But when it comes to Jesse Eisenberg's Lex
Luthor, I am not a fan.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:58.979
It feels like he's taken his Social Network
turn as Mark Zuckerberg and dialled the tics

00:01:58.979 --> 00:02:04.069
up to 11 in the misguided hope of pulling
off a performance as memorable as Heath Ledger's

00:02:04.069 --> 00:02:05.069
Joker.

00:02:05.069 --> 00:02:06.850
Sadly for us, he does not.

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:10.500
Cuts could definitely have been made to the
film's 2 and a half hour runtime, including

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:16.080
one especially ridiculous cameo, a plot-holey
subplot, and a needlessly-repeated flashback

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.860
that feels like the director and writers shouting
to us, 'hey, idiots, I know you have the memory

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:24.760
of a goldfish, so here's that bit you saw
earlier that you need to remember now to understand

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:25.760
this bit!'

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.040
Having said that, the script by Argo screenwriter
Chris Terrio and David S Goyer mostly moves

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:35.001
along well, and they deal with Batman's already
super-familiar origin story very neatly indeed.

00:02:35.001 --> 00:02:39.800
And I really liked the way the film cleverly
links back to the events of Man of Steel,

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.050
giving us a completely new angle on what happened.

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:47.020
But overall, I felt that the reasons for Batman's
dislike of Superman were better established

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:49.700
than the reasons for Superman's dislike of
Batman.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:53.880
There's no denying that BvS is a dark and
gritty film, but compared to Man of Steel,

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:57.950
you can sense they've made a concerted effort
to include more humour in the script.

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:02.430
Some of those efforts pay off nicely, though
a number fail to land, just as they did in

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:03.430
Superman's first outing.

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:08.850
Let me be clear on this: I do not need or
want DC to replicate Marvel's formula, but

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:12.190
remember that even amongst the darkness of
Nolan's and Burton's Batman movies, there

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:13.610
was still a spark of wit.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:19.160
There are some incredible and exciting action
sequences throughout, and director Zack Snyder

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:23.780
takes his time to build up to the big battle
advertised in the main title, which for me

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:29.030
was a plus as it allowed time to build both
tension and the characters' antagonism rather

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:31.970
than racing to the main event too quickly.

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:35.960
Props to stunt co-ordinators Damon Caro and
Tim Rigby and their stunt teams for their

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:40.130
work throughout, as well as sound editor Scott
Hecker and his department especially for their

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:42.470
standout work during the action sequences.

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:47.110
How much you enjoy a certain part of the action
will depend on what you think of wholly CG

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:52.260
creations that feel like riffs on the Hulk
meets King Kong via Godzilla and Ray Harryhausen's

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:53.260
creatures.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:57.670
BvS is pretty quickly cut by editor David
Brenner who keeps the pace up nicely through

00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:58.680
most of the movie.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:02.710
And there's some interesting use of slow-mo,
though in a few scenes it gets taken a bit

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:03.710
too far.

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:08.070
Still, I was glad that this time there wasn't
so much shaky-cam or the incessant zooming

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:12.100
in, zooming out, and zooming back in again
we had in Man of Steel, which at times in

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:15.270
that movie made me feel like I was watching
an episode of 24.

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:19.859
In terms of the look of the film and its sets,
I can't fault the work of production designer

00:04:19.859 --> 00:04:24.661
Patrick Tatopoulos, Art Director Troy Sizemore
or Set Decorator Carolyn Loucks and their

00:04:24.661 --> 00:04:29.130
teams who manage to create distinctive environments
for Batman, Superman, and Lex Luthor.

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:33.130
One of my biggest bugbears about BvS though
is the music.

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:38.040
It's incredibly overbearing and bombastic,
and feels like it's constantly trying to take

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:42.160
over each scene, especially during dramatic
moments and action sequences.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:47.010
The score's by Hans Zimmer, who scored Nolan's
Batman trilogy, and Junkie XL, who composed

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:49.700
the scores for Mad Max: Fury Road and Deadpool.

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:53.900
As fantastic as their work is on other movies,
what they did here just does not work, to

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:57.870
the point where it takes you out of the film
and endlessly batters you about the head.

00:04:57.870 --> 00:05:01.660
I really hope they change things up for future
DC outings.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:07.310
So, to sum up, Batman v Superman: Dawn of
Justice is a super-powered, super-packed affair

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:09.840
that brings plenty to the comic book movie
table.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:10.840
Is it perfect?

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:13.470
No, of course, it's not, far from it.

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:18.290
But it's also far from being a disaster, and
there's definitely much to like about it,

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:22.760
not least Zack Snyder and cinematographer
Larry Fong's visual flair, some incredible

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:26.580
action sequences, Ben Affleck's Batman, and
Gal Gadot's Wonder Woman.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:29.390
In fact, I much preferred it to Man of Steel.

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:33.630
Overall, BvS gives me hope for the nascent
DC Extended Universe.

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:35.730
Fingers crossed they live up to that.

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:39.060
Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice gets 3
out of 5 Flicks.

